A||||16||88formula||Polyurethane bushings and KYB Struts on 88||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||05-12-2000||07:43 PM||||I would like to know how much harder my 88 formula will ride with the polyurethane bushings and what improvement to expect from them in all four wheels? How good are the KYB struts and shocks? How does this stuff compare to the stock ride?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Eric||05-12-2000||08:22 PM||||I have KYBs on the front of my 88GT and they suck.  Maybe they have a lot of miles on them or something, but they just aren't right.  Save up for some Konis if you want performance.  <P>I may try poly bushings on  the rear and see how they feel.  There are a few rough roads on my daily commute and I don't want to beat the crap out of myself on the trip there. <P>I definatly  want to lower the front via lowering springs or spindles.  The later is to expensive for now.  As I recall, someone said you can use a lowering spring (Eibach?) from an eariler year Fiero and cut it down to the 88's spring size and get an inch or so lower.  Don't try it until I find out for sure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||gixxer||05-13-2000||12:30 AM||||Oi, Eric! Take a look at  <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com," TARGET=_blank>www.americancustomeng.com,</A> <BR>They list some Eibach springs for '88s(prolly modified).<BR>And they're on sale!<BR>I'm getting a bit tired of the stock wimpy  springs on my Formula myself.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||gixxer||05-13-2000||12:45 AM||||WHATS THIS?<BR>I'm a full member now, and I haven't been kicked off the board yet?<BR>I must be loosing my touch... ||||reg||11
Z||000004||turbo86se||05-13-2000||10:31 AM||||88Formula, I've got polyurethane bushings, Koni shocks and struts, ST lowering springs on my 86SE. It rides very smooth, not choppy at all. Right now with the 14" tires it doesn't handle great, but I've got new 16" wheels on the way!||||reg||6
Z||000005||88formula||05-14-2000||04:30 PM||||The Koni's are way too expensive. I just want something a little better than the stock shocks and struts. I'm not looking to autocross my fiero I just want it to have a ride a little firmer than stock without an over damped suspension. I don't want it so stiff that it slams over every imperfection in the road either.<P>I have talked to one person about the Prothane bushings and they said that the ride will not be adversely affected and that the car would react quicker to steering inputs and handle better. <P>Eric, why do you think the KYB shocks suck? If the factory shocks are OK than how can the KYB's suck?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||05-16-2000||05:42 PM||||La la la la||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||05-16-2000||06:13 PM||||I too would like to know about ride stiffness due to poly. <P>I have the same stuff in mind. KYB shocks and polyurethane bushings everywhere, on my '88 Formula. Anyone care to comment on KYB and/or poly ride quality on their Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric||05-16-2000||11:20 PM||||KYBs suck because I don't like them!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>Anything that I don't like sucks!  That being said, you would be fine with KYBs on your car.  They are better than stock, although not much better.  You will notice an improvement replacing old worn out shocks with new shocks.  I want Konis because they're in a whole different ballpark than the KYBs, which aren't racing shocks like the Konis, but are still okay to some people.  <P>I don't really know about the poly bushings.  Most people who use them say it really makes the car uncomfortable.  Many hot rodders use these and say they are fine and don't make the car bouncy or whatever.  I need a car for racing so my Fiero needs to handle.  But I also need a car to drive so my Fiero needs to be comfortable.  What I plan on doing is install poly bushings in the rear end first.  If I feel that those bushing in the front will jar me to death, I'll replace the worn out rubber units with new rubbers.  If the ride is good and more precise, I'll replace the front bushings with the polys.||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||05-16-2000||11:58 PM||||KYB shocks are all valved the same,so if you have an 1800 lb Datsun B-210,they'll perform different than in a 2800 lb 280ZX.Also they're a 50/50 valve proportion,and Fieros need alot more rebound control in front than most shocks will give.<BR>I'd try some Monroe's if I was really on the cheap.<P>I too think KYBs suck in most cars,if your car matches the qualities of the shock then you're good,otherwise it's just a compromise,and they're not THAT cheap either.<P>I like Konis because you can adjust the compression forces with a different fluid,and control the rebound with the adjustment mechanism.||||reg||3
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||05-17-2000||02:11 AM||||I have urethane bushing on the engine cradle, all suspension points and can tell you that it does not affect you ride that much at all. The big difference I felt was when I put the urethane on the torque strut, I felt a little more vibration from the enging but it was a hell of a lot better than the mush bushing there before. I was very happy with the urethane. And considering that urethane will probably out last the car a very good investment. I think. <P>As I understand 88s seemed to have gotten soft springs to improve the ride, so it wasn't so "harsh." Also I was under the impression that Konis discontinued Fiero suspension parts. Is this not true?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex4mula||05-17-2000||09:18 AM||||I have an 88 Formula with KYB and all poly suspension in the back and on sway bar in front. Ride is not harsh but more solid. You feel a little more imperfections on the road. My car almost doesn't roll. I like that. I did the KYBs first and did not found them really different than the Monroe before. The place were poly will really add vibration is in the dog bone. But you get used to it. Go for it. You will like the response.||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||05-17-2000||06:03 PM||||A lot of you guys are saying the KYB shocks and struts suck, but you don't say why ride wise. The Konies are four bills just for the rear and the KYB struts are a hundred and thirty. The Conies are for serious suspension buildups. If the KYB's suck and you can attest to that ride wise than what other choice do I have but to get monroe’s which are no better. <P>All I want is something that is as good or better than the OEM ones with a little firmer motion control. I don’t want to spend eight hundred bucks on shocks and struts. I will sell the car before I ever do something like that. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||05-17-2000||07:42 PM||||Same here. I just want a better setup than what I have right now. 12 year old suspension bushings, and what look like original shock/struts with 160K miles aren't the best either. What does everyone else have if not KYBs? I doubt we have 1,000 people running Konis on all four corners.||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||05-17-2000||10:29 PM||||Actually, price wise, the top end Monroes aren't cheap either. KYB is about the same price as these.<P>It all depends what you are doing with the car. For heavy race use you'll likely want Koni but for any normal use the top end monroes or the KYBs are probably fine. Keep in mind Monroe makes shock for racing applications. These people really do know what they are making and how to tune it.<P>The best thing to do is replace the shocks and struts first (Unless the bushings are toasted.) and see how it rides. then decide if you want polly.<P>Polly is not without it's problem either. For a daily driver I would use Moog rubbers in front for everything but the sway. I'd do the sway in polly. (Polly on the sway gives effect of the next stiffer bar, at least, for next to nothing.) <P>In the rear I'd do the cradle in poly on all fiero, but especially on manual tranny cars. on the 4 cyl i'd still do the rear control arms in rubber. I might do polly or rubber on the 6. Polly at the least on an 8.<P>Biggest problem with polly is it howls if it gets dry. Which it does all to often. Everyone thinks polly is so great for everything. They forget to take the condition of the bushings they replaced into acount. If Polly is compaired to NEW rubber bushings it loses much of it's appeal quickly.||||reg||1
Z||000015||ka4nkf||05-18-2000||09:58 PM||||I have monroe sensetrack shocks and they are no better than stock.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||05-19-2000||01:53 AM||||I have poly bushings, KYB shocks/struts, and Eibach springs. I went with the KYB's to keep the ride a little soft because I knew the springs would stiffen it up alot. I have rode in a car set-up like mine except he had the Koni's. The car handled like glue but as a daily driver and the imperfections in most city streets it was a bit harsh. If you plan on racing I'd go with Koni's. I haven't rode in a car that had original springs and Koni's so I'm not sure how the ride would be in that set-up. It sounds like that set-up would stiffen up the suspension but not as bad as using the stiffer springs. Just do what I did, Join a local club and talk to the other members and see how they set-up their suspensions and take a ride. See what you like and go that route. ||||reg||1
